INT. SALT MINE CONFERENCE CHAMBER, 400 METERS DOWN - DAY

A vaulted hall hewn from crystalline rock, lit by amber work lamps. The walls glitter. A corporate banner reads SYNERGY SUMMIT: DIGGING DEEPER, hung from two rusty hooks. Fifteen folding chairs face a flip chart. A catering table groans under an untouched platter of sandwiches.

MARGUERITE "MAGS" LUNDQUIST-BEY, fifties, the regional VP, stands at the flip chart with a laser pointer, hard-hatted and smiling. Seated in the front row: FERDINAND "FERDIE" OKAFOR, forties, a division head wearing a hard hat over a toupee. DOLORES PEÑA-FITZGERALD, sixties, legal counsel, holding a clipboard like a rosary. TYBALT CHANDRASEKHAR, twenties, an intern in an ill-fitting vest. And a STRANGER in a hard hat and a beige jacket, who sits at the back, notebook open, saying nothing.

MAGS
Team, thank you for coming down. Literally!

Nobody laughs. The stranger writes something.

FERDIE
Mags, is the elevator running?

MAGS
It's a hoist, Ferdie.

FERDIE
Is the hoist running?

MAGS
(brightly)
Let's circle back.

DOLORES
Margaret, I'd like it recorded that I objected to a location with no cell service.

MAGS
It's recorded.

DOLORES
By whom?

MAGS
By... the universe.

She clicks the laser pointer. A red dot lands on the flip chart: a pyramid labeled VALUE, with a pit beneath it labeled YOU.

TYBALT
(muttering)
That's not a good pyramid.

MAGS
Tybalt, did you have something to contribute?

TYBALT
No. Sorry. Just clearing my throat.

STRANGER
(from the back)
It's a very good pyramid.

Every head turns. The stranger doesn't look up from his notebook.

FERDIE
And you are?

STRANGER
Observing.

DOLORES
Observing what?

STRANGER
How long it takes.

MAGS
For what?

STRANGER
(writing)
Everything.

A silence settles over the hall. The amber lamps hum. A droplet of brine falls from the ceiling and lands on the sandwiches.

FERDIE
(to the room)
Does anyone know who this is?

TYBALT
(very small)
I think he's from corporate.

DOLORES
Which corporate?

TYBALT
The one that, um, bought us. Last Thursday.

Mags's smile falters. The laser pointer clicks off.

MAGS
Last Thursday.

TYBALT
There was an email.

MAGS
I didn't see an email.

TYBALT
It went to everyone.

FERDIE
I didn't see it either.

DOLORES
(dryly)
It would have been on the server.

MAGS
Which is...

DOLORES
Upstairs.

The stranger finally closes his notebook. He stands, tucks it into his jacket, and takes off his hard hat. A bald, patient head. A name badge: RAYMOND PELLETIER, ACQUISITIONS.

RAYMOND
Raymond Pelletier. Acquisitions. Please, continue.

MAGS
(faintly)
Raymond.

RAYMOND
You were on the pyramid.

MAGS
I was.

RAYMOND
It's very good. But I have to say, the pit is rather deeper than the pyramid is high.

He gives a small, polite nod and sits again. Mags stares at the flip chart for a long, long moment.

MAGS
(to herself)
Well. That's that.

She lays down the laser pointer. She sits beside Ferdie, who has slumped in his chair, toupee slightly askew. Dolores, without a word, puts her hand on Tybalt's shoulder.

Raymond opens his notebook again. The lamps hum.

RAYMOND (CONT'D)
(to the room, gently)
Does anyone want a sandwich?

Nobody moves. Then Tybalt raises his hand.
